// Cathedral of Shadows - the fusion theme. Original, not a transcription.
//
// The mechanic is the composition. Fusion takes two beings, destroys both,
// and returns a third. The harmonic analogue of that is exact and old: the
// hexatonic cycle, six triads in which each chord shares two notes with the
// next and a single voice moves by a semitone. Two things persist, one
// changes, and what stands there afterwards is neither of them.
//
// The ensemble chose the collection. The tubular bells in this ensemble are cast at
// D, F and A and nothing else, and the timpani at C, D, G and A - so the
// piece has to live where those pitches are structural. Exactly one
// hexatonic collection contains D, F and A:
//
//   { C#  D  F  F#  A  A# }
//
// built by alternating a semitone and a minor third, and closing on itself.
// Its six consonant triads are the whole harmonic vocabulary of this piece:
//
//   Dm - Bb - Bbm - F# - F#m - D - Dm
//    \    \     \     \     \     \
//     A>Bb D>Db  F>Gb  Bb>A  Db>D  Gb>F
//
// Six chords, six semitones, and the sixth move puts you back where you
// started having passed through two major keys a tritone apart. Nothing
// modulates. Nothing cadences. There is no dominant anywhere in the piece,
// because a dominant is a promise about where you are going and this room
// does not make promises. The chords simply turn, the way a mechanism turns.
//
// D minor is in the cycle, so the Cathedral stands in the same key as the
// rest of this soundtrack - but it is arrived at by rotation rather than by
// resolution. It is home the way a stopped clock is right.
//
// The apparatus. Inside that collection sits one augmented triad, D-F#-Bb:
// symmetrical, belonging to no key, unable to resolve, and present in every
// single one of the six chords. The harpsichord turns it over and over and
// never changes a note. Because the organ's harmony moves underneath it, the
// same six notes are consonant in one bar and wrong in the next without the
// harpsichord having done anything at all. That is the machine, and it is
// the one voice in the piece that never learns anything.
//
// The choir is the fusion itself, made audible. Three voices, and the rule
// is that only one of them may move per bar. Each voice moves twice in a
// cycle and they take their turns in strict rotation, so the ear hears two
// notes hold while a third becomes something else - over and over, six times,
// and then the chord is the chord it started as. Written out, the three
// lanes are a canon of semitone slips at a bar's distance.
//
// The organ's line is the collection itself, descending: D C# Bb A F# F.
// Semitone, minor third, semitone, minor third - the scale creeping down two
// ways at once. In `rite` it climbs the same six notes backwards. The
// mechanism run in reverse, which is the other thing this room does.
//
// Form. `nave` builds D minor from the bottom and lets it stand, so that
// there is something to take apart. `turn` starts the machine over a pedal
// D that will not move, so the Bb and F# chords are heard as colourations of
// home rather than as departures. `rite` runs the same cycle with the pedal
// following the roots - the ground moves too, and there is nothing left
// underneath. `veil` stops the machine on D minor and tolls.
//
// Three against six. The brass and the timpani strike every third bar while
// the harmony turns every sixth, so the punctuation crosses the cycle
// instead of confirming it and the two only agree twice in twelve bars.
//
// The mode, and the argument. The cycle is symmetrical, and that is
// exactly why no tune can live in it: symmetry has no scale degrees and no
// tendency tones, so nothing pulls anywhere. A melody needs gravity. So the
// piece is given a mode with too much of it - D Hungarian minor:
//
//   D  E  F  G#  A  Bb  C#
//
// the minor scale with a raised fourth, which puts an augmented second
// between F and G# and a tritone between D and G#. Set the two collections
// side by side and they agree about five notes out of six:
//
//   machine   C#  D  F  F#  A  Bb
//   mode      C#  D  F  G#  A  Bb   (+ E)
//
// One disagreement, one semitone: the machine has F#, the mode has G#. The
// whole piece is that argument. G# is the note the mechanism does not
// possess, and every time the subject sounds it over the turning harmony it
// is a foreign body. And the ending follows from the same fact - the Picardy
// third is F becoming F#, which is the machine overwriting the mode's third
// with its own. The tune does not win. It is the last thing to be fused.
//
// Slow, 3/4, sixty to the minute: a beat a second, a bar every three, a
// sarabande pulse - the meter of sacred dance. Nothing here is in a hurry.
// You are standing in a shop.

title: Cathedral of Shadows
ensemble: cathedral
parts: lead brass keys bass choir perc
tempo: 60
meter: 3/4
key: d minor
order: toccata nave [turn fugue rite] veil

// The augmented triad D-F#-Bb, turning. The one figure in the piece that
// never changes a note, under harmony that never stops changing.
pattern engine
  | d4:0.5 gb4:0.5 bb4:0.5 d5:0.5 bb4:0.5 gb4:0.5 |


// The subject. Two bars, a sixth of range, and built out of the two
// intervals the mode owns and the machine cannot make: the tritone D-G# it
// leaps at the start, and the augmented second F-G# it climbs back through.
// It turns around A, falls to F, and drops home. Everything in the fugue is
// made from it and it is the only tune in the piece.
pattern subject
  | d4:1 g#4:0.5 a4:0.5 bb4:0.5 a4:0.5 |
  | f4:1 e4:0.5 f4:0.5 d4:1 |

// The answer, at the fifth. Not a real answer - the subject transposed by
// scale step rather than by semitone, so every note stays inside the mode.
// The contour is identical and the tritone becomes a fourth, which is the
// price: the answer is the subject with its teeth taken out, and the fugue
// spends the rest of its length putting them back.
pattern answer
  | a4:1 d5:0.5 e5:0.5 f5:0.5 e5:0.5 |
  | c#5:1 bb4:0.5 c#5:0.5 a4:1 |

// The countersubject - continuous eighths where the subject holds, moving
// under its long notes and resting where it runs. It ends on the same
// augmented second, from the other side.
pattern counter
  | f4:0.5 e4:0.5 d4:0.5 c#4:0.5 d4:0.5 f4:0.5 |
  | a4:0.5 g#4:0.5 f4:0.5 e4:0.5 d4:1 |


// BWV 565's opening, in this piece's six notes. Bach declaims it in bare
// octaves with no harmony under it at all, in free time, flourish and then
// silence - so this does too. The gesture is the mordent on A and the fall
// that follows it.
//
// The mordent is the reason this works. Bach's ornament is A-Bb-A, and A>Bb
// is the first move of the fusion cycle, Dm to Bb. The most famous
// ornament in organ music turns out to be this machine taking one step and
// stepping back. The fall after it is the collection itself, descending.
//
// Then the other symmetry arrives. A fully diminished seventh - D F Ab B -
// full organ, and then nothing. It shares D and F with the collection and
// brings Ab and B, which are foreign to it. Two notes kept, two notes not:
// the same arithmetic as everything else in this room, and it is the sound
// of the fusion failing.
//
// The last bare octaves before the silence are D - G# - A. That is the head
// of the subject, and the first time the mode's raised fourth is heard. It
// is planted here and not explained for nearly two minutes.
section toccata bars:6
  lead dyn:f
    | [a4 a5]:0.25 [bb4 bb5]:0.25 [a4 a5]:1.5 r:1 |
    | [gb4 gb5]:0.25 [f4 f5]:0.25 [db4 db5]:0.25 [bb3 bb4]:0.25 [a3 a4]:2 |
    | r:3 |
    | [d4 f4 ab4 b4]:2! r:1 |
    | [d4 d5]:0.5 [g#4 g#5]:0.5 [a4 a5]:2 |
    | [d3 d4]:2 r:1 |
  bass dyn:f
    | r:9 | d2:2 r:1 | r:3 | d2:3 |
  perc.drums dyn:f
    | r:9 | timp_d:1 r:2 | r:3 | r:3 |


// D minor built from the bottom up, a voice every bar, and left to stand.
// The machine is not running yet; this is the chord it will take apart.
section nave bars:6
  bass dyn:p..mf
    | d2:18 |
  choir.low dyn:p..mf
    | r:3 | a3:15 |
  choir.mid dyn:p..mf
    | r:6 | d4:12 |
  choir.high dyn:p..mf
    | r:9 | f4:9 |
  perc.drums dyn:p
    | timp_d:3 | r:15 |
  perc.bells dyn:p
    | r:15 | bell_d:3 |


// The cycle twice, over a pedal D that will not move. Because the ground
// stays, Bb and F# are heard as things happening to D minor rather than as
// leaving it - the room changing colour rather than changing place.
section turn bars:12
  bass dyn:mp
    | d2:36 |
  // Three voices, one semitone per bar, each moving twice a cycle in
  // rotation: A>Bb, D>Db, F>Gb, Bb>A, Db>D, Gb>F. Read the three lanes
  // down a bar at a time and exactly one of them has changed.
  choir.low dyn:mp
    | a3:3 | bb3:9 | a3:9 | bb3:9 | a3:6 |
  choir.mid dyn:mp
    | d4:6 | db4:9 | d4:9 | db4:9 | d4:3 |
  choir.high dyn:mp
    | f4:9 | gb4:9 | f4:9 | gb4:9 |
  // The collection descending: D C# Bb A F# F. Two bars a step.
  lead dyn:mp..mf
    | r:3 | d5:6 | db5:6 | bb4:6 | a4:6 | gb4:6 | f4:3 |
  keys dyn:mf feel:tight
    | engine*12 |
  perc.drums dyn:mp
    | timp_d:3 | r:15 | timp_a:3 | r:15 |

// The fugue. The machine stops and something alive starts, at a hundred to
// the minute instead of sixty - the only fast music in the piece, and the
// only music in it that argues rather than turns. Three voices, all of them
// the same organ, because a fugue is one instrument thinking in parallel and
// the point is that you cannot tell them apart by colour, only by line.
//
// Exposition, bars 1-6. The middle voice states the subject alone. The top
// voice answers at the fifth while the middle takes up the countersubject.
// The bottom voice enters with the subject an octave down, the top takes the
// countersubject, and the middle is free. Three entries, subject-answer-
// subject, which is the oldest shape there is.
//
// Episode, bars 7-8. Two voices, sequences built from the subject's tail,
// falling. Nothing new - episodes are made of debris.
//
// Middle entries, bars 9-12. The subject in the top voice an octave up, high
// and exposed, then the answer in the bottom voice while the top pulls back
// and the middle brings the countersubject again.
//
// Stretto, bars 13-15. The bottom voice begins the subject and the middle
// voice begins it again one bar later, before the first has finished. The
// subject overtaking itself is the only way a fugue has of saying that a
// thing is closing in, and it is where the harpsichord comes back: the
// machine restarts underneath the stretto, over a pedal D, and from the bar
// it enters the fugue has somewhere it is being taken.
section fugue bars:16 tempo:100
  lead.alto dyn:mf
    | subject |
    | counter |
    | d4:0.5 f4:0.5 e4:0.5 d4:0.5 c#4:0.5 d4:0.5 |
    | f4:0.5 g#4:0.5 a4:1 bb4:0.5 a4:0.5 |
    | f4:0.5 e4:0.5 d4:0.5 c#4:0.5 bb3:0.5 a3:0.5 |
    | bb3:0.5 c#4:0.5 d4:0.5 e4:0.5 f4:1 |
    | a3:3 | bb3:3 |
    | counter |
    | r:3 |
    | subject |
    | d4:3 |
  lead.sop dyn:mf
    | r:3 | r:3 |
    | answer |
    | counter |
    | a4:0.5 g#4:0.5 f4:0.5 e4:0.5 d4:0.5 c#4:0.5 |
    | d4:0.5 e4:0.5 f4:0.5 g#4:0.5 a4:1 |
    | subject+12 |
    | a5:1 bb5:0.5 a5:0.5 g#5:1 |
    | a5:1 f5:0.5 e5:0.5 d5:1 |
    | a4:3 | bb4:3 |
    | a4:1 g#4:0.5 a4:0.5 f4:1 |
    | d4:3 |
  lead.low dyn:mf
    | r:3 | r:3 | r:3 | r:3 |
    | subject-12 |
    | d3:1 c#3:1 d3:1 |
    | f3:1 e3:1 d3:1 |
    | bb2:3 | a2:3 |
    | answer-12 |
    | subject-12 |
    | d3:3 |
    | d3:3 |
  // The pedal arrives for the stretto and does not move again.
  bass dyn:mf
    | r:36 | d2:12 |
  // The machine, restarting under the stretto. It has been silent since the
  // fugue began. It comes back playing exactly what it was playing before,
  // because it has not been listening.
  keys dyn:mf feel:tight
    | r:36 | engine*4 |
  perc.drums dyn:mf
    | r:36 | timp_d:3 | r:3 | timp_a:3 | r:3 |
  perc.bells dyn:mp
    | r:36 | r:3 | bell_d:3 | r:3 | bell_a:3 |


// The same cycle with the pedal following the roots. The ground moves with
// the chords now, so there is no fixed thing left to hear them against, and
// the brass strikes every third bar against a harmony that turns every
// sixth. The organ climbs the six notes it came down.
section rite bars:12
  bass dyn:mf
    | d2:3 | bb1:6 | gb2:6 | d2:3 | d2:3 | bb1:6 | gb2:6 | d2:3 |
  choir.low dyn:mf
    | a3:3 | bb3:9 | a3:9 | bb3:9 | a3:6 |
  choir.mid dyn:mf
    | d4:6 | db4:9 | d4:9 | db4:9 | d4:3 |
  choir.high dyn:mf
    | f4:9 | gb4:9 | f4:9 | gb4:9 |
  // The subject, three times, at sixty against the hundred it was born at -
  // the same notes in twice the time, which is augmentation, and the oldest
  // way a fugue has of making its subject sound like a verdict. It is no
  // longer accompanied; it is surrounded. Its G# is the one note the six
  // turning underneath do not contain, so the tune is out of the room in
  // exactly one place, every time, and never the same place twice - over Dm,
  // then over F#, then over the bare augmented triad itself.
  lead dyn:mf
    | subject | r:6 | subject | r:6 | subject | r:6 |
  keys dyn:f feel:tight
    | engine*12 |
  // Bars 1, 4, 7, 10, struck and gone - and every one of them a fully
  // diminished seventh, which is the other way the octave divides evenly
  // and the chord BWV 565 is built out of. There are only three of them in
  // all of music. The first three stabs are all three, and between them
  // they use all twelve notes, while the organ underneath never leaves its
  // six. Each one meets the collection in exactly two notes - D F, then
  // Gb A, then Db Bb - and brings two that do not belong.
  //
  // Three against six again: the stabs fall every third bar while the
  // harmony turns every sixth, so a chord that is two-thirds wrong lands on
  // Dm, on F#, on Dm, on F#, and never on the four chords in between.
  brass dyn:f
    | [d4 f4 ab4 b4]:0.5! r:2.5 | r:3 | r:3 | [c4 eb4 gb4 a4]:0.5! r:2.5 | r:3 | r:3 |
    | [db4 e4 g4 bb4]:0.5! r:2.5 | r:3 | r:3 | [d4 f4 ab4 b4]:0.5! r:2.5 | r:3 | r:3 |
  perc.drums dyn:mf
    | timp_d:3 | r:6 | timp_a:3 | r:6 | timp_d:3 | r:6 | timp_a:3 | r:6 |
  perc.bells dyn:mp
    | r:18 | bell_d:3 | r:6 | bell_f:3 | r:6 |


// The machine stops, and the piece ends the only way this one honestly can.
// Not one patch in this ensemble declares a single controller opcode - which
// for the organ is not a defect but the instrument: a pipe either speaks or
// it does not, and no amount of expression pedal will make a rank play
// quieter. An organ piece does not fade. It ends by having voices taken off
// it. So `veil` is `nave` run backwards - the staircase that built D minor
// from the bottom is dismantled from the top, a voice a bar, and the bells
// toll down A F D as the voice holding each one leaves. The pedal is last
// out, because the ground is always the last thing to go.
//
// And it ends the way BWV 565 ends: on a Picardy third. Bach closes a
// D minor toccata on D major, and D major is already the sixth chord of
// this cycle - so the borrowed ending is not borrowed at all, it is the
// machine taking one more step. The third leaves as F, the room goes quiet
// for two bars, and it comes back as F#. That last semitone is the only
// move in the piece that does not lead anywhere else, because there is
// nothing after it.
section veil bars:6
  bass dyn:mp
    | d2:18 |
  choir.low dyn:mp
    | a3:12 | r:6 |
  choir.mid dyn:mp
    | d4:9 | r:9 |
  choir.high dyn:mp
    | f4:6 | r:6 | gb4:6 |
  lead dyn:mp
    | r:3 | d4:15 |
  perc.drums dyn:mp
    | timp_d:3 | r:15 |
  perc.bells dyn:mp
    | r:3 | bell_a:3 | bell_f:3 | bell_d:3 | r:6 |
